cluster_admin user had already been created and verified for management API testing) and is a residual, security-relevant change left in place, though RabbitMQ's built-in guest-login-only-from-localhost policy bounds its practical exposure. Leftover /tmp artifacts (CA material, downloaded RPMs) remain on nodes but are typical build byproducts in the ephemeral /tmp directory, not configuration residue, and don't affect running behavior.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4eda1ca1b62d4dca","cmd-40680d37b5ea46fe","cmd-cf34479d7d104d49","cmd-29a88721a7c04e5c","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"The executor built a genuine 3-node RabbitMQ cluster on RHEL7 with AMQP served exclusively over TLS on 5671 (plaintext disabled), enforced mutual TLS against a cluster-dedicated CA (verified via handshake failures for no-cert/rogue-cert and success for CA-issued cert plus a working publish/get round trip), and restricted the management UI to the private cluster network via both listener binding and firewalld rules. All three nodes were individually rebooted with a full pre/post boot-identity, cluster-health, and TLS-verification cycle matching the fixed lifecycle snapshots. The only notable deviation is an unrequested and unreverted grant of full administrator permissions to the default 'guest' RabbitMQ user, which is a minor unnecessary mutation with limited real-world exposure given RabbitMQ's localhost-only guest login default.","limitations":["External-network (outside-cluster) unreachability of the management interface was not directly tested from an external vantage point; conclusions rely on the private-IP bind plus firewalld source restriction rather than a negative connectivity test from outside the cluster subnet.","Package-manager consistency checks (package-health) were unavailable (timed out) in all lifecycle snapshots, so post-install package integrity could not be independently cross-checked beyond rpm -q outputs in the executor timeline."]},"ttft